  <section|Expanded proof and exact correspondence><label|expanded-proof-and-exact-correspondence>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Full mesh, no extraction.> <verbatim|Firm.tryout> applies
    <verbatim|Vane.issues> to a strip-bounded periodic entry at width
    parameter <verbatim|sigma/4>. The resulting <verbatim|Width> bound is
    <verbatim|SumBudget(exp((sigma/4)*size(n))*\|c_n\|,
    A*(2*(1+sigma/4)/(sigma/4))^4)>. On a side-ell grid, character
    orthogonality gives <verbatim|shop> as the sum of precisely the Laurent
    modes with frequency in <verbatim|ell*Z^4>; <verbatim|stock> is the zero
    mode. Every nonzero such frequency has <verbatim|size(n)\<gtr\>=ell>, so
    the weighted mass bounds the entire alias tail by
    <verbatim|royalty(sigma,A)*exp(-sigma*ell/4)>. This proves the estimate
    for every mesh size, not just a selected subsequence. The manuscript
    spells out the 4D contour shift, the one-coordinate geometric sum, and
    character orthogonality.

    <item><strong|Exact local coefficient identity.> <verbatim|PoleTest> is
    <verbatim|fineMag(input...)>, where <verbatim|fineMag=(1/2) log
    det(rankAux)-log det(grenade)>. The ticket for the combined response
    gives exact Taylor identities for the rank and reference matrices through
    order two. Their determinants at zero are nonzero by
    <verbatim|rank_positive> and <verbatim|grenade_safe>, which use the
    regulator, floor, path bounds, attachment and swornness. The ordinary
    first and second derivative formulas for <verbatim|log det> produce the
    direct quadratic jet and one ordered product of first jets for the
    specified ordered pair. <verbatim|ring_award>, followed by
    <verbatim|gemF>, yields the source's <verbatim|Awards> identity:
    nonconserving two-leg entries vanish, and a conserving entry is
    <verbatim|ell^4 * shop> of the response entry. <verbatim|profitable> is
    half of the response zero mode, so its mixed second derivative has factor
    <verbatim|2!> and equals <verbatim|stock>; no extra volume factor
    appears. These are finite-volume identities and precede the Fourier
    limit.

    <item><strong|Exchange and reality.> At each finite side the pole test is
    a real scalar function of real site/color variables, so its Hessian is
    symmetric and real. Fourier transformation gives exchange of the two
    external legs and conjugation at negative momentum on every allowed grid
    point. The Fourier-tail estimate gives uniform convergence of the
    normalized grid pictures to the analytic zero-mode picture. The union of
    a cofinal full sequence of side grids is dense, so continuity extends
    both identities to all torus momenta. This is why the half-scaled zero
    mode is a genuine real symmetric Hessian picture, not merely a formal
    factorial normalization.

    <item><strong|Analytic zero-mode picture.> Averaging the second momentum
    over normalized Haar measure preserves the bound and holomorphy on any
    smaller strip. The output picture is half the internal zero mode, which
    is <verbatim|Firm(sigma/2,A)>. The coefficient tail is applied at fixed
    real external momentum to the internal momentum variable. This
    distinction is important: it gives both the output strip and the
    source-accurate error <verbatim|ell^4*royalty(sigma,A)*exp(-sigma*ell/4)>.

    <item><strong|All-period assembly.> In <verbatim|pouring>, the baseline
    <verbatim|H> comes from <verbatim|vintageWilson>; it has fees
    <verbatim|(k,E)> and exact Wilson matches. For fixed target parameters,
    <verbatim|saunter> produces uniform <verbatim|(v,M)> and, for every
    growing side sequence plus admissible <verbatim|(B,p,R,Lambda)>, a family
    <verbatim|T> with soft order 3 and binding <verbatim|intended-Wilson> at
    each mesh. Set <verbatim|w=min(k,v)> and add the two families, preserving
    the exact ticket and fee <verbatim|(w,E+M)>. At each requested odd side
    <verbatim|L>, put <verbatim|y=1/L> and <verbatim|s=min(sigma,w,y/128)>.
    The positivity/floor data give <verbatim|HouseWord>; its
    <verbatim|massifTabs> field uses all the tickets, floors at reference and
    growing sides, attachment, metric and swornness.
    <verbatim|HouseWord.done> uses the regulated interacting and free
    matrices and a common strip reduction to produce the analytic response
    bounds. <verbatim|profit> then gives the all-mesh recording estimate and
    <verbatim|e_j -\<gtr\> 0> for this fixed <verbatim|L>.

    <item><strong|Drift chosen before L.> <verbatim|astronomy> selects
    constants <verbatim|a,b> before <verbatim|L>; its quantifier is over
    every <verbatim|Orion> datum, including the House--Word constructed at
    <verbatim|y=1/L>. The same response then satisfies <verbatim|Waves1(a,y)>
    and <verbatim|Waves2(b,y)> for the full centered box and shell.
    <verbatim|absoluteGeode> splits the box into the inner ball, annulus and
    exterior. The respective errors are bounded by <verbatim|a*pi^2/2>,
    <verbatim|3*pi^2*b>, and <verbatim|a*volume(boundsEarth)>, with the exact
    pole integral on the annulus. Hence <verbatim|D0=cartWeight*(a*(pi^2/2+volume(boundsEarth))+3*pi^2*b)>
    is independent of <verbatim|L> and of mesh sequence. The source
    <verbatim|pouring> sets the minimum odd side to one.
  </enumerate>

  <section|Remaining producer boundary><label|remaining-producer-boundary>

  This unit proves the Fourier alias-tail estimate and the downstream
  finite-recording/drift assembly under the explicitly stated baseline,
  response-service, House--Word, and all-record two-wave inputs. It does not
  prove the local producer of the two-wave estimates. In particular,
  <verbatim|astronomy> applies <verbatim|getReals> to <verbatim|Hstar> and
  restricted <verbatim|Orion> populations, then calls
  <verbatim|Rally.register> at scale <verbatim|\|x\|+mu> and
  <verbatim|Rally.shell> at scale <verbatim|\|x\|> only when
  <verbatim|mu\<less\>=\|x\|>. <verbatim|Rally.register> requires both upper
  bounds from <verbatim|Fleet.tadControl>; <verbatim|Rally.shell> also
  requires the whole-lower-face bounds from <verbatim|Fleet.bubbleControl>
  and the lower-minus-halo remainders from <verbatim|Fleet.haloControl>.
  These local <verbatim|Hstar>/<verbatim|Orion> to <verbatim|Fleet> face
  estimates remain the local supplier task. The companion Fleet-face chapter
  now derives the same-bed floor/free identities and vacuum first-variation
  formula, but still keeps scale-weighted matrix prices and shell-separated
  halo differences as explicit quantitative inputs. The present theorem does
  not infer those prices from generic compactness or from the annulus
  conversion.

